Chapter: WcetProbe
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Driver for empirical WCET validation. Three punctual functions in
 distinct shapes: clamp-add is near-straight-line arithmetic through
 the min and max safe builtins, classify is a branch ladder whose
 dynamic path is strictly shorter than its emitted body, and tiny
 carries an explicit 64-instruction budget and enough body to stay
 out of the inliner, so its entry breakpoint is observable. Under
 build/wcet-validate.ps1 the codex-vm -wcet mode single-steps each
 invocation and asserts two hard gates: the observed per-invocation
 instruction count never exceeds the declared budget, and never
 exceeds the CDX6010 static count -- the static count is a decode of
 the finished body bytes and punctual code cannot loop, so any
 dynamic path is a subset. As a plain battery test the program
 simply prints deterministic results.

Section: Punctual Functions

  punctual clamp-add : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  clamp-add (a) (b) =
    let s = a + b
    in min (max s (-1000)) 1000

  punctual classify : Integer -> Integer
  classify (n) =
    if n > 100 then 3
    else if n > 10 then 2
    else if n > 0 then 1
    else 0

  punctual 64 tiny : Integer -> Integer
  tiny (n) =
    let a = bit-and n 255
    in let b = bit-or a 16
    in min (b + 3) 200

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("ca1: " & show (clamp-add 500 700))
    print-line-uni ("ca2: " & show (clamp-add (-800) (-900)))
    print-line-uni ("ca3: " & show (clamp-add 5 7))
    print-line-uni ("cl1: " & show (classify 500))
    print-line-uni ("cl2: " & show (classify 50))
    print-line-uni ("cl3: " & show (classify 5))
    print-line-uni ("cl4: " & show (classify (-5)))
    print-line-uni ("t1: " & show (tiny 41))
  end
